# What `docker build backend/` may see. A different file from .gitignore and a different
# job: .gitignore decides what is committed, this decides what is uploaded to the engine
# and can therefore reach a layer, or invalidate one.
#
# NOT .gitignore semantics. Patterns are anchored at the context root and `*` does not
# cross `/`, so a bare `__pycache__` — the spelling .gitignore uses — leaves every nested
# one behind; anything that must match at depth is written `**/`. Negation also runs the
# other way round: the last matching rule wins and `!` really does pull a path back out of
# an excluded directory, which is the only reason the allow-list below is legal at all.
#
# Deny everything, then name the three build inputs. An allow-list rather than a list of
# things to skip, because a list of things to skip only ever excludes the ones somebody
# thought of: it is what makes PLAN-BACKEND §9's ".dockerignore excludes .env" true by
# construction. The stale backend/.venv, tests/, dist/ and every tool cache are out
# because nothing names them in.
#
# The cost is one line per new build input, paid in the commit that adds it: 1.9 adds
# `!alembic.ini` and `!alembic` beside the two COPY lines it adds to the Dockerfile, and a
# forgotten negation is a failing COPY at build rather than a silently empty directory.
# `!alembic` the directory — a negation naming files inside an excluded directory
# (`!alembic/**/*.py`) re-includes nothing at all.
*
!pyproject.toml
!poetry.lock
!src
!alembic.ini
!alembic

# src/ is the one directory named back in, and a working tree leaves things inside it that
# are not source. Not hygiene for its own sake: `poetry build` packages whatever sits
# beside the modules, so an editor's `settings.py~` is installed into site-packages as a
# second, complete, greppable copy of the one module that names the tracker key — which is
# the property tests/test_secret_containment.py exists to deny, and cannot see, because it
# scans the source tree and not the image.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*~
**/*.sw[op]

# Already excluded by `*` above, and structurally too: the monorepo keeps .env at the
# repository root while this context is backend/. These two lines are PLAN-BACKEND §9's
# requirement written where a reviewer greps for it, and they guard the one case the
# layout does not — a developer who runs the service from backend/ and leaves a
# backend/.env behind. Both spellings, because `.env.*` does not match `.env`.
.env
.env.*
